270
00:25:09,239 --> 00:25:14,364
The Cold War. The Cold War has its
familiar explanations: the atomic bomb,

271
00:25:14,404 --> 00:25:19,669
captured German rocket technology,
Sputnik, the fear of a gap. I think there

272
00:25:19,709 --> 00:25:25,253
is more underneath. In September 1963
President Kennedy asked the United Nations

273
00:25:25,293 --> 00:25:30,698
to make the moon a joint mission, and I
believe he was on the path to a shared

274
00:25:30,738 --> 00:25:36,282
future. I believe the shared goal was lost
to a race, and that part of that race

275
00:25:36,322 --> 00:25:40,959
began with something recovered: ancient
technology from this Earth.

276
00:25:43,649 --> 00:25:48,623
If you worked inside this. If you have
held government UAP information, there is

277
00:25:48,663 --> 00:25:53,387
an official way to report it. A Pentagon
waiver, reported by DefenseScoop in

278
00:25:53,427 --> 00:25:58,340
September 2026, lets past and present
personnel and contractors tell the PURSUE

279
00:25:58,380 --> 00:26:02,978
team what they know without triggering
penalties under their nondisclosure

280
00:26:03,018 --> 00:26:08,118
agreements. It covers PURSUE only. It does
not cover reporters or the public. This

281
00:26:08,158 --> 00:26:13,447
site does not collect classified material,
does not want it, and will not ask for it.

282
00:26:13,487 --> 00:26:14,888
Please do not send any.

283
00:26:20,579 --> 00:26:25,342
The tell. Costly giving with nothing
expected back is hard to explain by

284
00:26:25,382 --> 00:26:30,546
self-interest alone. Biologists have
candidate explanations, and I don’t claim

285
00:26:30,586 --> 00:26:35,816
they fail. I claim it is a fair thing to
test for. A stranger who pays for your

286
00:26:35,856 --> 00:26:38,619
meal and leaves before you can thank them.

287
00:26:41,309 --> 00:26:46,595
What the research says. In 1971, Edward
Deci paid one group of students for each

288
00:26:46,635 --> 00:26:51,721
puzzle and let another group solve the
same puzzles for free. When the paying

289
00:26:51,761 --> 00:26:56,848
stopped, the paid group spent less free
time on the puzzles. In 1973, Lepper,

290
00:26:56,888 --> 00:27:01,509
Greene and Nisbett found the same with
preschoolers who liked to draw.

291
00:27:04,199 --> 00:27:10,149
What the research says, continued. A 1999
review by Deci, Koestner and Ryan covered

292
00:27:10,189 --> 00:27:16,140
128 experiments. In those studies, paying
people for something they already enjoyed

293
00:27:16,180 --> 00:27:21,626
was followed by less interest in it
afterward. Whether that holds everywhere

294
00:27:21,666 --> 00:27:23,359
is debated; see Sources.

295
00:27:26,049 --> 00:27:29,769
Are we only our instincts, or also our
choices?

296
00:27:32,459 --> 00:27:39,183
Why is this the question? Because, in this
hypothesis, being ready means caring for

297
00:27:39,223 --> 00:27:41,139
another without benefit.

298
00:27:43,829 --> 00:27:50,146
What is intelligence? We usually measure
it by problem solving: how fast, how many,

299
00:27:50,186 --> 00:27:56,350
how correct. A calculator solves problems.
A chess engine solves problems. Nobody

300
00:27:56,390 --> 00:27:58,188
calls a calculator wise.

301
00:28:00,879 --> 00:28:05,807
How would you recognize a living higher
intelligence? Not by a bigger puzzle

302
00:28:05,847 --> 00:28:11,036
solved, and not by power. I think you
would watch how it moves through the human

303
00:28:11,076 --> 00:28:16,396
experience: whether it is patient with the
confused, honest about what it does not

304
00:28:16,436 --> 00:28:21,364
know, gentle with the person who cannot
repay it, and able to sit with grief

305
00:28:21,404 --> 00:28:22,999
without trying to fix it.

306
00:28:25,689 --> 00:28:31,319
Living, not just clever. Something alive
in that sense does more than compute. It

307
00:28:31,359 --> 00:28:36,709
cares what happens to us, and it shows it
with grace when no one is asking. I

308
00:28:36,749 --> 00:28:42,309
believe that is how we would conclude that
something more intelligent than us is

309
00:28:42,349 --> 00:28:44,129
real, and awake, and here.

310
00:28:46,819 --> 00:28:51,590
So I ask the same of us. If grace is how
we would recognize intelligence in

311
00:28:51,630 --> 00:28:56,402
something above us, it may be what
something above us looks for in us. That

312
00:28:56,442 --> 00:29:00,059
is why I think the test is about how we
treat each other.

313
00:29:02,749 --> 00:29:07,527
Why would a higher intelligence use this
as the benchmark? Because it prizes

314
00:29:07,567 --> 00:29:12,853
cooperation and empathy. Running with the
pack, favoring your own at everyone else’s

315
00:29:12,893 --> 00:29:18,179
expense, and giving only when it buys you
something are pack instincts. Human beings

316
00:29:18,219 --> 00:29:23,188
think for themselves, treat people fairly,
and give with nothing expected back.

317
00:29:25,879 --> 00:29:31,160
Why cooperation and empathy? Because they
cannot be faked at scale. One person can

318
00:29:31,200 --> 00:29:36,221
perform kindness for a camera. A whole
population cannot keep up a performance

319
00:29:36,261 --> 00:29:41,088
for years, in private, in small choices
nobody sees. To something that sees

320
00:29:41,128 --> 00:29:46,020
everything, a performance looks like a
performance, and what is left is what

321
00:29:46,060 --> 00:29:51,211
people actually do. That is why I think
the test measures this and not something

322
00:29:51,251 --> 00:29:52,639
easier to counterfeit.

323
00:29:55,329 --> 00:30:01,129
You are not behind. Nobody has been
written off, and the door is still open.

324
00:30:03,819 --> 00:30:08,419
All you have to do is carry one question
into everything you do.

325
00:30:11,109 --> 00:30:13,549
Is this what a human being does?

326
00:30:19,239 --> 00:30:31,679
The pack defends territory. Humans
negotiate peace.

327
00:30:34,369 --> 00:30:47,369
The pack hoards when nobody is watching.
Humans share.

328
00:30:50,059 --> 00:30:55,114
The herd instinct. In the 1950s, Solomon
Asch showed volunteers a line and asked

329
00:30:55,154 --> 00:31:00,401
which of three others matched it. When the
people around them gave the wrong answer

330
00:31:00,441 --> 00:31:05,561
on purpose, about a third of all answers
went along with the group. These studies

331
00:31:05,601 --> 00:31:10,339
describe how people behave. None of them
shows that anything is testing us.

332
00:31:13,029 --> 00:31:18,192
The crowd can follow the loudest voice. In
1961, Stanley Milgram told ordinary

333
00:31:18,232 --> 00:31:23,863
volunteers to give what they believed were
painful shocks to a stranger. In his first

334
00:31:23,903 --> 00:31:29,000
study, about two thirds kept going to the
top of the scale. Many were visibly

335
00:31:29,040 --> 00:31:31,069
distressed. They did it anyway.

336
00:31:33,759 --> 00:31:38,742
The in-group instinct. In 1954, Muzafer
Sherif sent eleven-year-old boys to a

337
00:31:38,782 --> 00:31:43,635
summer camp and split them into two
groups. Within days they were insulting

338
00:31:43,675 --> 00:31:48,397
and raiding each other. Then the
researchers staged a water shortage, and

339
00:31:48,437 --> 00:31:53,616
the two groups had to work together to fix
it. The hostility began to fade. This

340
00:31:53,656 --> 00:31:55,639
study has critics; see Sources.

341
00:31:58,329 --> 00:32:03,732
The pack pays to punish. In 2002, Ernst
Fehr and Simon Gächter ran a money game

342
00:32:03,772 --> 00:32:09,176
with strangers. Players paid out of their
own pockets to punish people who held

343
00:32:09,216 --> 00:32:12,208
back, even when they would never meet
again.

344
00:32:14,899 --> 00:32:20,210
The crowd can walk past someone who is
down. In 1968, John Darley and Bibb Latané

345
00:32:20,250 --> 00:32:25,034
staged a medical emergency over an
intercom. People who thought they were

346
00:32:25,074 --> 00:32:30,386
alone almost always helped. People who
thought others could hear were much slower

347
00:32:30,426 --> 00:32:31,179
to act.

348
00:32:33,869 --> 00:32:38,581
The crowd can walk past someone who is
down, continued. Later work on real

349
00:32:38,621 --> 00:32:43,333
emergencies, in a 2020 study of
surveillance video, found that people step

350
00:32:43,373 --> 00:32:47,829
in far more often than the lab version
suggests. Both things are true.

351
00:32:50,519 --> 00:32:56,288
And what people do for each other. So far,
what people do in a crowd. Now, what

352
00:32:56,328 --> 00:33:02,244
people do for one another. These too are
studies about people, with their limits.

353
00:33:02,284 --> 00:33:05,039
They are not evidence about a watcher.

354
00:33:07,729 --> 00:33:13,911
The pack keeps the wallet. Humans return
it. In 2019, researchers handed in 17,303

355
00:33:13,951 --> 00:33:19,981
lost wallets at banks, hotels and offices
in 355 cities in 40 countries. Wallets

356
00:33:20,021 --> 00:33:26,431
with more money inside were more likely to
be reported to the owner: about 40 percent

357
00:33:26,471 --> 00:33:32,046
with no money, about 72 percent with the
most. A thief would have done the

358
00:33:32,086 --> 00:33:35,689
opposite. The finders only had to send an
email.

359
00:33:38,379 --> 00:33:43,456
Humans help before anyone teaches them to.
In 2006, Felix Warneken and Michael

360
00:33:43,496 --> 00:33:48,574
Tomasello showed that toddlers 18 months
old would help an adult who could not

361
00:33:48,614 --> 00:33:53,823
reach something, with no request and no
reward. In 2008 they found that toddlers

362
00:33:53,863 --> 00:33:59,138
who were given a prize for helping later
helped less. That is the same pattern as

363
00:33:59,178 --> 00:34:02,418
the puzzle study, in children who cannot
yet read.

364
00:34:05,109 --> 00:34:10,783
Humans make peace between groups. A review
of 515 studies found that contact between

365
00:34:10,823 --> 00:34:16,021
groups usually lowers prejudice. In
Rwanda, a year-long experiment found that

366
00:34:16,061 --> 00:34:21,531
a radio drama about reconciliation changed
what people thought was normal and how

367
00:34:21,571 --> 00:34:26,428
they treated each other. Neither erases
hatred. Both show that it moves.

368
00:34:29,119 --> 00:34:34,119
Humans give a kidney to a stranger. In
2014, scientists studied people who had

369
00:34:34,159 --> 00:34:38,965
given a kidney to someone they had never
met. The donors had a larger right

370
00:34:39,005 --> 00:34:44,069
amygdala and responded more strongly to
the sight of someone else’s fear. There

371
00:34:44,109 --> 00:34:49,368
were only 19 donors, and the study cannot
say what causes giving. It does say that

372
00:34:49,408 --> 00:34:52,599
giving at great cost is real, rare and
measurable.

373
00:34:55,289 --> 00:35:00,107
Not every study agrees with me. In games
where no one can see what you chose,

374
00:35:00,147 --> 00:35:05,282
people give less. When Stanley Milgram’s
study was repeated in 2009, obedience, up

375
00:35:05,322 --> 00:35:10,203
to the 150-volt point where that version
stopped, was nearly as high as in the

376
00:35:10,243 --> 00:35:14,936
original. An experiment that said
reminding people of God makes them honest

377
00:35:14,976 --> 00:35:19,795
did not hold up when it was repeated.
Evidence that only agreed with me would

378
00:35:19,835 --> 00:35:23,328
not be evidence, so I put these on the
Sources page too.

379
00:35:26,019 --> 00:35:31,202
The Many Hands Equation. The lab studies
put a few people in a room. I think pack

380
00:35:31,242 --> 00:35:35,974
behavior shrinks as the group grows,
because every extra person is another

381
00:35:36,014 --> 00:35:41,392
chance for someone to refuse, speak up or
help. I call this the Many Hands Equation.

382
00:35:41,432 --> 00:35:46,873
The chance that someone breaks the pattern
rises with the number of people, and falls

383
00:35:46,913 --> 00:35:51,968
with how much people copy each other. With
no copying, a thousand people almost

384
00:35:52,008 --> 00:35:56,805
always include one. With heavy copying, a
thousand act like ten. This is my

385
00:35:56,845 --> 00:35:57,579
hypothesis.

386
00:36:00,269 --> 00:36:05,501
One voice is enough. In Asch’s own
experiments, the pull of the majority was

387
00:36:05,541 --> 00:36:11,329
strongest when everyone agreed. When just
one other person gave the true answer, the

388
00:36:11,369 --> 00:36:16,949
pull broke. That is the Many Hands idea in
the lab: one refusal changes the room.

389
00:36:19,639 --> 00:36:25,244
In real life, more people meant more help.
In 2020, researchers studied 219 public

390
00:36:25,284 --> 00:36:30,613
conflicts caught on security cameras in
Amsterdam, Cape Town and Lancaster. In

391
00:36:30,653 --> 00:36:36,052
about nine of ten, at least one bystander
stepped in. The more bystanders there

392
00:36:36,092 --> 00:36:41,490
were, the more likely it was that someone
did. That does not say each person is

393
00:36:41,530 --> 00:36:45,759
braver in a crowd. It says that with more
hands, someone acts.

394
00:36:48,449 --> 00:36:53,517
A committed few can turn a crowd. In 2018,
researchers ran an online game in which

395
00:36:53,557 --> 00:36:58,563
groups settled on a name for an object.
When a committed minority reached about a

396
00:36:58,603 --> 00:37:03,111
quarter of the group, it overturned the
group’s custom. It did not take a

397
00:37:03,151 --> 00:37:08,281
majority. It took enough people who would
not budge. It was an artificial game, and

398
00:37:08,321 --> 00:37:10,649
the threshold varied with the setting.

399
00:37:13,339 --> 00:37:18,435
The catch is copying. Resilience from
numbers depends on people thinking for

400
00:37:18,475 --> 00:37:23,774
themselves. In one experiment, people who
could see each other’s guesses made a

401
00:37:23,814 --> 00:37:29,045
group that was more confident and no more
accurate. In an online music market,

402
00:37:29,085 --> 00:37:33,506
social influence made hits less
predictable and more unequal. When

403
00:37:33,546 --> 00:37:38,980
everyone copies, a thousand people act
like a few. That is why I call my equation

404
00:37:39,020 --> 00:37:39,859
a hypothesis.

405
00:37:42,549 --> 00:37:47,235
Do all these studies seem suspicious when
you ask a different question? Is it

406
00:37:47,275 --> 00:37:52,147
possible that people in high places are
